Subject: Advertise the colour depth the ZRLE encoder actually uses
Base: GNOME Remote Desktop 50.2
License: GPL-2.0-or-later (upstream-derived)
Status: local candidate; not upstream-submitted or release-qualified

LibVNCServer's rfbGetScreen sets depth = 8 * bytesPerPixel, so a BGRX framebuffer
is announced in ServerInit as depth 32 although only 24 bits carry colour. Its
ZRLE encoder, however, picks the 3-byte CPIXEL form whenever the colour bits fit
in three bytes, ignoring the announced depth. A viewer that keeps the server's
pixel format (RealVNC Viewer for Android does; the desktop viewer negotiates
depth 24 first and so never sees this) follows the RFB specification, expects
4-byte CPIXELs at depth 32, and abandons the stream with "bad xrle data" a few
hundred milliseconds after the first frame, then reconnects for ever. Announce
depth 24, which is what the pixels are and what the encoder emits.

--- a/src/grd-session-vnc.c
+++ b/src/grd-session-vnc.c
@@ -113,6 +113,15 @@
    */
   swap_uint8 (&rfb_screen->serverFormat.redShift,
               &rfb_screen->serverFormat.blueShift);
+
+  /*
+   * Only 24 of the 32 bits carry colour, and LibVNCServer's ZRLE encoder emits
+   * 3-byte CPIXELs for such a format regardless of the announced depth. A viewer
+   * that keeps our format decides the CPIXEL size from the depth we announce, so
+   * it must be 24, or the stream is unreadable to it ("bad xrle data").
+   */
+  rfb_screen->depth = 24;
+  rfb_screen->serverFormat.depth = 24;
 }
 
 static void
